AI Agents: Claude Code and Codex
********************************

.[perex]
An AI coding agent writes PHP code fast and in its own way. After each of its edits, DressCode fixes the file to match your project's coding standard and, like a good tailor, tells the agent right away what it altered: a diff of the rewrite, the imports it removed, and the findings that remain, with advice on what to do about them.


Installation
============

In Claude Code, two commands are enough:

```shell
/plugin marketplace add nette/agent-plugins
/plugin install php-fixer@nette
```

From then on, after every edit of a PHP file, the `php-fixer` plugin runs DressCode with the project's configuration, that is, the nearest `dresscode.neon`. **Where there is no configuration, it formats by the Nette standard**, whatever style the project is written in; so write one beforehand with [`dresscode init` |generate-configuration], which measures it from your code.

Codex uses the same plugin, installed with `codex plugin add php-fixer@nette`; you approve its hooks once in `/hooks`. When DressCode is not installed in the project or globally, the `install-php-fixer` skill installs it.

The hook covers files written by the `Edit` and `Write` tools in Claude Code and by `apply_patch` in Codex. It does not see a file the agent writes with a shell command (`Bash`); such a file is fixed only by the next edit or by `dresscode fix`.


What the Agent Gets
===================

When nobody tells the agent what changed in the file it is editing, a well-known loop starts: the agent writes a `use`, the hook deletes it as unused, the agent adds the code that needed the import and wonders where the error came from. Or it edits another place based on the text it remembers, and the edit fails because the file looks different now. The hook therefore uses the `agent` output format, which tells the model in plain sentences what happened:

/--pre .[terminal]
DressCode changed `src/Cart.php`; the changes below are already in the file:

- removed the import of `Acme\Shop\Payment\Gateway`: nothing used it; if you meant to use it, add the import in the same edit as the use.

```diff
@@ -12,7 +10,7 @@

 	public function add(string $name, int $price): void
 	{
-		$this->items[] = ["name" => $name, "price" => $price];
+		$this->items[] = ['name' => $name, 'price' => $price];
 	}


```

Left in `src/Cart.php`, at the lines of the file as it is now:
- line 19: the `@` operator must not be used (`mistakes.errorSuppression`). Fix it; where the current code is intended and you know why, keep it with a comment on its line, `// @ <why>`, giving the real reason for `<why>`; otherwise tell the user.
\--

- **For a clean file, the hook prints nothing**, so it does not flood the context.
- **A rewrite comes as a diff** of what is already in the file, so the next edit targets the right text. When the rewrite is too long to fit in the message, the agent gets no diff, only an instruction to read the file again.
- **A removed import is named**, with the advice to add it in the same edit as its use.
- **Every violation says what to do with it**, and a risky fix also says why it was not made and that the change must keep the intended behavior.
- **An exception is offered only in the form the project approves.** Where the project allows exceptions approved by a comment (the key [`suppressionComments` |suppressions-and-baseline]), the agent gets an example of that comment. `dresscode:ignore` is never offered to the agent.
- **The format leaves out warnings about the configuration**, because the agent can do nothing with them.


The Style of the File
=====================

The hook also passes DressCode the text of the file before the edit (`--before`). Any decision the file broke already before the edit is the style of the file, not the agent's mistake. DressCode leaves it as it is and tells the agent to follow the style of the file there:

/--pre .[terminal]
Before the edit `src/Legacy.php` did not follow `indentation.unit`, `literals.quotes`, so DressCode leaves them as the file writes them; write the code there in the style of the rest of the file.
\--

So when the agent edits one method of a file that does not meet the standard yet, the rest of the file is not reformatted. A small commit gets nothing the agent did not write, and a file indented with spaces stays indented with spaces.

On the other hand, whatever the edit broke is fixed, wherever it is: an import whose last use the edit removed, or lines the edit wrapped in a new block. A new file belongs entirely to the agent, so it is fixed completely. The rest of the project is a job for `dresscode fix`, which you run deliberately and commit separately.


Without a Hook
==============

An agent or a tool that can run a command after writing a file can run the same command itself:

```shell
dresscode fix src/Cart.php -f agent --skip-excluded --before /tmp/Cart.before.php --lines 12-14
```

Thanks to `--skip-excluded`, the command does not touch a file that the configuration excludes in `excludePaths`, for example a test fixture the agent wrote to. Without `--before`, the whole file is fixed; `--lines` only narrows the output.


Upgrading with an Agent
=======================

After an [upgrade of libraries |upgrade], you are left with a list of places that a human must rewrite, and every message in it says what to write instead of the old API. A list like that is a perfect job for an agent. With the `agent` format, the agent gets a diff of what DressCode rewrote and, for every remaining finding, what to do with it:

```shell
dresscode upgrade today -f agent
```

Unlike the terminal output, the `agent` format does not suggest another run, so run the command again until it rewrites nothing. The agent gets the [estimate and the plan of an upgrade |upgrade-plan#How Much Work an Upgrade Takes] as data, for example from `dresscode upgrade plan php 8.4 --format json`; the same goes for running the plan, for `upgrade tasks` and for `upgrade status`. These commands have no `agent` format. In the `json` format, DressCode asks no questions, not even in a terminal, so turn on the types with `--types`, and allow `upgrade run` to raise the versions in `composer.json` with `--yes`; what the document carries is described in [Command Line |cli#upgrade]. `init` and `migrate` give the agent data too: `dresscode init --format json` takes the recommended answers and returns the questions it would have asked under `instructions`.


Fewer Risky Fixes
=================

The hook does not make risky fixes; it only reports them. To keep as few of them as possible, the key [`nameResolution: certain` |php-functions-in-namespaces#name-resolution] belongs in `dresscode.neon` too: with it, the fixes that depend on a function being global are no longer risky, and the hook makes them itself. [Types |phpstan-types#enable] do the same for the fixes that depend on the type of a value.

